//? Title: Muller root iteration
//? Subtitle: Actual parabolas, callback scheduling and the real-discriminant clamp
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Locate a zero of a scalar function from three starting coordinates using only
function values. Muller iteration fits a quadratic and chooses a numerically
favorable root update. We inspect a hand-checkable quadratic, retain the
original cubic and exponential examples, and distinguish residual acceptance,
iteration caps and numerical stops. The native real-valued discriminant clamp
does not supply a complex-root method or a global convergence guarantee.
)

\toc

> Fit a parabola in local coordinates

At the three current points, define h0=x1−x0 and h1=x2−x1. Their divided
differences d0 and d1 determine the quadratic Q(h)=a*h squared+b*h+c in local
coordinate h=x−x2, with a=(d1−d0)/(h0+h1), b=a*h1+d1 and c=f(x2). Solving Q(h)=0
gives the cancellation-aware update

$$
x_{new}=x_2-\frac{2c}{b\pm\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}.
$$
\alttext(The new point subtracts two times the current function value divided by a selected quadratic-root denominator.)

Choose the sign making the denominator's magnitude larger; the native tie rule
chooses the minus branch. For a finite nonzero denominator this avoids
subtracting nearly equal terms in that denominator. It does not make every
earlier coefficient computation overflow-safe or guarantee that the selected
root is useful. The retained real variant replaces a negative discriminant by
zero. That changes the quadratic root problem and invalidates an unqualified
appeal to complex Muller convergence.

For a smooth simple root, sufficiently close distinct iterates and the
appropriate unmodified root branch, quadratic interpolation gives next error
proportional to the product of the preceding three errors at leading order. The
corresponding local-order equation is p cubed=p squared+p+1, whose positive root
is about1.839. Those assumptions, rather than a short falling residual plot,
justify the local order. Multiple roots, poor starts, clamps and degeneracies
need not behave that way.

> One real quadratic proposal

For f(x)=x squared−2 and seeds0,1,2, the fitted coefficients are exactly
a=1,b=4,c=2. The raw discriminant is8, so no clamp is needed. The larger
denominator gives a next point near the positive square root of two. The native
record supplies every coefficient and the proposed point; no report-side Muller
loop is used.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/muller_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::muller_trace([](double x) {
        return x * x - 2;
    }, 0, 1, 2);
    auto &s = t.steps.front();
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("a", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual local parabola\na={} / b={} / c={}", s.a, s.b,
                       s.c)).node("b", {7, 0},
                                  std::format("Actual discriminant {}\nchosen denominator {:.6f}",
                                              s.discriminant_raw, s.denominator)).node("c", {14, 0}, std::format("Actual next point\n{:.12f}", s.next)).edge("a", "b").edge("b", "c");
    std::print("| actual a | actual b | actual c | raw discriminant | clamp | actual denominator | next " "point |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\n| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | " "{} |\n",
               s.a, s.b, s.c, s.discriminant_raw, s.clamped, s.denominator,
               s.next);
    std::print("\nActual callbacks: {}, updates: {}, stop: {}.\n",
               t.run.evaluations, t.run.updates,
               dm::algorithms::muller_stop_name(t.run.stop));
    d.save_svg("build/muller_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual a | actual b | actual c | raw discriminant | clamp | actual denominator | next point |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 4 | 2 | 8 | false | 6.82842712474619 | 1.414213562373095 |

Actual callbacks: 4, updates: 1, stop: residual.
\image(figures/muller_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual quadratic coefficients and selected finite update.)
\alttext(The unmodified discriminant8 leads to the positive square-root update.)

> Retain the original cubic and exponential examples

The original cubic is x cubed−x−2 with seeds1,1.5,2. It has a root between1
and2, where its derivative3x squared−1 is positive, so that interval contains
exactly one root. The original exponential problem is exp(x)−2 with seeds0,0.5,1
and known root ln2. The native default tolerance is a strict absolute function
residual below1e−10. It is not a bound on coordinate error without additional
derivative information.

The plot below records actual default-tolerance cubic inspections. Compatibility
iterations count one-based loop residual inspections; actual updates and
callback calls are reported separately. If the last initial seed already passes,
the legacy iteration count is one but no update occurs.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/muller_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto cubic = [](double x) { return x * x * x - x - 2; };
    auto t = dm::algorithms::muller_trace(cubic, 1, 1.5, 2);
    auto e = dm::algorithms::muller_run([](double x) {
        return std::exp(x) - 2;
    }, 0, .5, 1);
    std::print("| original problem | native root | residual | compatibility iterations | actual updates | " "actual callbacks | converged |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n| cubic | " "{} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n| exponential | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",
               t.run.result.root, t.run.result.residual,
               t.run.result.iterations, t.run.updates, t.run.evaluations,
               t.run.result.converged, e.result.root, e.result.residual,
               e.result.iterations, e.updates, e.evaluations, e.result.converged);
    std::print("\nExponential root minus log2: {}.\n",
               e.result.root - std::log(2.));
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.steps.size(); ++i) {
        x.push_back(i);
        y.push_back(std::log10(std::max(std::abs(t.steps[i].f[2]), 1e-16)));
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, y, "actual cubic inspections", {124, 58, 237}, 6).title(
        "Original cubic: actual default-tolerance residuals").x_label(
        "actual residual-inspection index").y_label("log10 max(abs residual,1e-16)").save_svg("build/muller_original.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| original problem | native root | residual | compatibility iterations | actual updates | actual callbacks | converged |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
| cubic | 1.5213797068045676 | 0 | 5 | 4 | 7 | true |
| exponential | 0.6931471805599465 | 2.220446049250313e-15 | 5 | 4 | 7 | true |

Exponential root minus log2: 1.2212453270876722e-15.
