> Numerical stops and a function with no real root

The function x squared+1 has no real zero. Its real-variant run can clamp
negative discriminants and still stop without convergence; clamping does not
manufacture a solution. A constant nonzero function produces a zero denominator.
Repeated interpolation coordinates can stop with a coincident-node status when
an update is needed. Zero updates still evaluates all three initial points and
performs a final residual check.

Controls and seed finiteness are checked before callbacks. Nonfinite callback
returns throw a domain error; nonfinite produced spacing, coefficients or
updates throw overflow errors. Callback exceptions and earlier side effects
propagate. A zero tolerance disables strict residual acceptance, even at a
callback zero. The optional trace owns five seed/control scalar units and one
unit per actual callback/inspection record, default1000000; zero disables.
Admission is online and a later quota failure can follow earlier callbacks, with
no partial returned history.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/muller_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<std::string> labels{"no real root", "constant", "zero cap",
                                    "initial root"};
    std::vector<dm::algorithms::MullerRun> runs{dm::algorithms::muller_run([](double x) {
        return x * x + 1;
    }, -1, 0, 1), dm::algorithms::muller_run([](double) {
        return 1.;
    }, 0, 1, 2), dm::algorithms::muller_run([](double x) {
        return x * x * x - x - 2;
    }, 1, 1.5, 2, 1e-10, 0), dm::algorithms::muller_run([](double x) {
        return x - 1;
    }, 0, .5, 1)};
    std::vector<double> updates;
    std::print("| case | actual stop | converged | actual callbacks | actual updates | actual clamps |\n| " "--- | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < runs.size(); ++i) {
        auto &r = runs[i];
        updates.push_back(r.updates);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", labels[i],
                   dm::algorithms::muller_stop_name(r.stop), r.result.converged,
                   r.evaluations, r.updates, r.clamped_discriminants);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(updates, labels, "updates");
    p.title("Real Muller controls: stops and actual work").x_label(
        "function/control case").y_label("applied updates").save_svg(
        "build/muller_controls.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| case | actual stop | converged | actual callbacks | actual updates | actual clamps |
| --- | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| no real root | coincident-nodes | false | 5 | 2 | 2 |
| constant | zero-denominator | false | 3 | 0 | 0 |
| zero cap | iteration-limit | false | 3 | 0 | 0 |
| initial root | residual | true | 3 | 0 | 0 |
\image(figures/muller_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual work and termination outcomes for explicit numerical controls.)
\alttext(A clamped run without a real root and a zero-denominator run remain failures; an initial accepted root needs no update.)

> Conclusion and references

The quadratic example explains the actual proposal, while the two original
problems show finite residual acceptance. Callback histories and cap checks give
honest work counts. The real-discriminant clamp and explicit numerical failure
states limit any convergence claim; none of these unbracketed runs supplies a
general root-existence or coordinate-error certificate.

[Muller, A method for solving algebraic equations using an automatic computer (1956)|https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-1956-0083822-0]
introduces quadratic interpolation for root solving. The
nonnegative-discriminant clamp, strict residual rule, compatibility iteration
labels and capture contract specify the native real variant.

